Subject: An interpreted caller enters a compiled callee through its native entry

What this does
  The unit's table gains a native-entry pointer per function (`nfn`, NULL when the
  function has none), bound into the bytecode as `aot_nfn`. The interpreter's
  `call` and `call_method` handlers send a compiled callee to `qjs_aot_call_light`
  when it is light OR has a native entry; there, a callee with a native entry is called
  through it after the caller's frame is sealed, instead of building a light frame
  and entering the unit's frame-taking entry. The helper is inlined into the two
  handlers so its 16-byte result is not read back through memory it was just written
  to as two words (a store-forwarding stall that was half of the handler's time).
  QJS_AOT_ABI is bumped (the table entry changed size).

Why we need it
  An interpreted caller of a compiled function paid for a frame the compiled body
  never needed (frameless callees forward straight to their native entry), and a
  closure-reading function, which the engine does not call light, took the
  generic call path.

